        "proof_or_evidence": "Fourier identity (exact): for |A|=r sectors, J(A,E) = (1-r/7)^(k-1) + sum over NONTRIVIAL integer relations sum_{e!=0} m_e e=0 of prod_e c_{m_e}(A), where the avoid-indicator Fourier coeffs satisfy c_0=1-r/7, |c_m(A)| <= r|sin(pi m/7)|/(pi|m|) <= r/(pi|m|), c_m=0 when 7|m (per-m single-sector magnitudes computed: 0.138,0.124,0.103,0.078,0.050,0.023,0). The e=0 factor is identically 1 (avoids A always). Single stranger-peel (E=E'+{w}, V=total variation of F=prod_{E'} avoid-indicator): RIGOROUS bound |J(A,E)-(1-r/7)J(A,E')| <= r*V/(6w), with V<=14*sum(E') via BV: |hatF(n)|<=V/(2pi|n|), n=mw, summed against |c_m|<=r/(pi|m|) gives r*V/(2pi^2 w)*sum 1/m^2 = r*V/(6w). NUMERICALLY VERIFIED: r=2 stranger factor -> (1-r/7)=5/7=0.71429 exactly, residual decays ~1/w (w=7:diff+1e-5; w=521:diff+3e-4), reproducing HYP-2610's '5/7 factor, converges by N>=61'. Propagation: |L_y(E)-L_inf| <= sum_{r>=1}|y_r|C(6,r) eps_r; budget needs per-A eps < 0.00692 (k=8), 0.0239 (k=9), 0.0269 (k=10) [the sum|y_r|C(6,r) constants are 48, 14.33, 14.33]. The partition reconciliation: dilated APs {0,D,...,(k-1)D} have gcd=D, are NOT primitive; dividing by gcd returns consec (spread k-1) -> they live in the FINITE CHECK, consistent with scale-invariance THM-531. Artifact: 04-computation/lrc14_angleB_dissociation_closure.py; output 05-knowledge/results/lrc14_angleB_dissociation_closure.out.",
        "remaining_gap": "The clean dissociation/spread DICHOTOMY does NOT hold as hoped. CONCRETE FINDING: there exist MANY primitive (gcd=1), wide-spread (>20) k=8 sets in which EVERY element participates in a small (height-2) relation — i.e. NO dissociated stranger exists (3873 of 20000 sampled; e.g. [0,3,5,16,28,30,33,35], [0,4,12,15,20,21,25,31]). The dissociation peel does NOT cover these wide-but-structured sets. So the partition '{bounded-spread B} (finite) vs {has dissociated stranger} (peel)' is INCOMPLETE — there is a third pocket: primitive, spread>B, no dissociated stranger (partial APs / Sidon-complement-like sets). These are handled here only EMPIRICALLY (all have L_y ~0.04-0.18 << cap, far from extremal), not by a self-contained bound. To close: either (a) prove every primitive k-set with spread > B (explicit B, ~16) either has a dissociated stranger OR is itself a near-AP that can be bounded by a Weyl-discrepancy argument on its longest AP-substructure; or (b) replace 'dissociated stranger' with a weaker quantity (e.g. additive energy / number of small relations) that is small for ALL wide primitive sets and feeds the same Fourier tail. The explicit separation threshold for the dissociated branch grows like w ~ 14*sum(E')*max_r/(6*eps) (e.g. for k=8, eps<0.0069: w >> ~2000*sum(E')); a tighter V(F) than the crude 14*sum(E') (actual jumps were 10 vs bound 56 for E'=[0,1,3]) would shrink this.",
