          "evidence": "I re-derived everything independently with fractions.Fraction and cross-checked against the repo engine 04-computation/lrc14_empty_sector_distribution_kps.py. Scripts: 04-computation/verify_angleB{,2,3,4,5}.py; outputs in 05-knowledge/results/verify_angleB{2,3,4,5}.out.\n\nCONFIRMED EXACTLY:\n- y_r coefficients: I re-derived y_r as the inverse-binomial transform of g(t) and verified the reconstruction g(t)=sum_r y_r C(t,r) for all t. k=8: y=(1,-1,1,-9/10,3/5,0,0); k=9,10: y=(1,-13/18,4/9,-1/6,0,0,0). Exactly as claimed.\n- Step 1 closed form L_inf = sum_r y_r C(6,r)(1-r/7)^(k-1): matched the claimed exact fractions to the bit. k=8: 40573/823543=0.049266; k=9: 2616535/17294403=0.151294; k=10: 8830611/40353607=0.218831. Denominators 7^7,7^9,7^9 confirm exponent k-1.\n- The CORRECTED EXPONENT k-1 (not k): decisively confirmed. For a strongly dissociated k=8 set E=[0,1,11,113,1129,3457,7919,9973], the exact breakpoint-engine J(A) for |A|=r matches (1-r/7)^(k-1) far better than (1-r/7)^k: r=1 J=0.3364 vs (k-1)=0.3399 vs (k)=0.2914; r=2 J=0.0941 vs 0.0949 vs 0.0678; r=3 J=0.0183 vs 0.0199 vs 0.0114. The e=0 pinning argument is correct.\n- Fourier single-sector magnitudes 0.138,0.124,0.103,0.078,0.050,0.023,0 (m=1..6, then 0 at m=7): exact. c_m=0 when 7|m: confirmed. The r-sector triangle bound r|sin(pi m/7)|/(pi m) holds for r=1,2,3 (verified against exact contiguous-A coefficients).\n- Propagation constants sum_{r>=1}|y_r|C(6,r): exactly 48 (k=8), 43/3=14.333 (k=9,10), and per-A budgets 0.006922, 0.023928, 0.026900 — all match claimed 48/14.33/14.33 and 0.00692/0.0239/0.0269.\n- Exhaustive k=8 over all 3431 primitive sets with 0 in E, |E|=8, max<=14: consec is the UNIQUE global maximizer, L_y=2633/7350=0.358231 < cap 0.3815, ZERO ties, NO set reaches cap. Reproduces the claim's bounded-spread finite check exactly.\n- Dissociated sets converge to L_inf and stay well under cap (k=8 'mild' set L_y=0.0506 vs L_inf=0.0493; all geometric families << cap). Scale-invariance/dilated-AP reasoning is consistent.\n\nDISCREPANCY (why PARTIAL, not CONFIRMED):\n- Step 3's stated 'decisive' figure is NUMERICALLY UNDERSTATED. Claim: 'max L_y over primitive-spread>16 sets is ~0.179 (k=8)'. My adversarial hunt found wide primitive sets far above this: E=[0,1,2,3,4,5,6,17] gives L_y=787/3400=0.231471 (spread 17), and consec-7-plus-one-outlier reaches L_y=0.257 at d=14. So the '~0.179 ceiling' is wrong; the true wide-set ceiling is higher (>=0.257 for single-outlier configs). This does NOT break the closure conclusion — every wide set found still sits well below cap 0.3815 (margin >=0.12) — but the specific quantitative claim that wide sets max at ~0.179 is false as stated.\n- The analytic closure remains genuinely partial (consistent with STRONG-PARTIAL): the stranger-peel bound r*V/(6w) with V<=14*sum(E') degrades as the set grows and only beats the per-A budget for sufficiently large separation w. No explicit threshold W* is exhibited above which the budget is provably met for ALL primitive sets; the 'wide' regime is still covered by numerics (Step 3), whose ceiling figure is itself understated. So the gap between the bounded-spread finite check and the asymptotic bound is not yet rigorously closed by explicit constants.\n\nBottom line: every exact/closed-form sub-claim (y_r, L_inf, exponent k-1, Fourier magnitudes, propagation constants, consec uniqueness over bounded spread) is CONFIRMED. The qualitative conclusion (dissociated/wide primitive sets have L_y < cap) holds in all my tests. But the headline Step-3 numeric ('~0.179') is wrong, and the analytic half does not yet close the gap with explicit constants — so the overall closure is PARTIAL, matching the STRONG-PARTIAL self-assessment."
