      {
        "angle": "Angle C — Literature import: do the 2026 LRC papers' AP-extremality / finite-reduction methods transfer to proving \"consec maximizes L_y = E[g(N)]\" (HYP-2607)?",
        "progress": "REFRAME-ONLY",
        "result": "Located and characterized all four target sources. KEY FACTS: (i) Sungkawichai-Trakulthongchai \"Eleven, twelve, and thirteen lonely runners\" (arXiv:2604.23906, Apr 2026) proves LRC for k=10,11,12 = 11/12/13 runners. THEIR k=13 (=14 runners, i.e. LRC(14)) is NOT covered — they stop at k=12. So LRC(14) is still genuinely open in the literature; our route is not preempted. (ii) Their proof is a DIVISIBILITY-SIEVE BARRIER, not a covering/measure bound, so it does NOT transfer to bounding L_y. (iii) Their ONE AP-specific algebraic result (Prop 4.1) is an existence-of-good-time statement, NOT an extremality-of-AP-for-a-measure statement. (iv) Tao (arXiv:1701.02048) is the ONLY source with a transferable shape: an L^2/second-moment bound on the covering-multiplicity function PLUS a structural lemma forcing a length-~n arithmetic progression at the near-extremum. (v) Goddyn-Wong constructions live in a bounded-length AP — matches our tight-locus={AP, Goddyn-Wong T5}.",
        "proof_or_evidence": "SOURCES (verified via fetch): Sungkawichai-Trakulthongchai, arXiv:2604.23906 (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.23906, HTML https://arxiv.org/html/2604.23906); precursor Trakulthongchai \"Nine and ten lonely runners\" arXiv:2511.22427; Tao \"Some remarks on the lonely runner conjecture\" arXiv:1701.02048 + blog https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2017/01/10/.\n\n(A) 2604.23906 SCOPE: their k = (#runners)-1; they prove k in {10,11,12}. LRC(14) is k=13, which they do NOT prove. The title's \"thirteen runners\" = k=12. CONFIRM our LRC(14) is the first open case.\n\n(B) Their machinery (extracted from HTML, exact statements):\n - Def 2.1 \"(k,p,l)-proper\": exists i with gcd(l,v_1..hat v_i..v_k)>1, OR exists t in (1/(lp))Z with ||t v_i||>=1/(k+1) for all i.\n - Def 2.3 J(k,p) = set of v in Z_{p,1}^k not eventually (k,p)-proper.\n - Cor 2.5 / Lemma 2.2 (finite reduction): if LRC(k-1) holds and J(k,p)=emptyset, any counterexample u to LRC(k) has p | u_1...u_k.\n - Lemma 2.6 (Malikiosis-Santos-Schymura): any counterexample has u_1...u_k < B_k := (C(k+1,2)^{k-1}/k)^k.\n - Prop 2.7: verify J(k,p)=emptyset for a finite prime set P with prod_{p in P} p >= B_k ==> LRC(k). (Table 1: 61 primes k=10, 70 k=11, 98 k=12.)\n This is a DIVISIBILITY barrier: emptiness mod p forces p | product, finitely many primes overwhelm the size bound. There is NO measure/volume/empty-sector count anywhere (fetch confirmed: \"paper does not explicitly measure clustering or empty-sector volumes\").\n\n(C) Their ONLY AP statement = Prop 4.1 (polynomial method): \"Let k+1 be an odd prime. For all v in N_k [v has >=1 zero coord], there are s,r in Z_{k+1}^x with s v + r(1,2,...,k) in {1,...,k-1}^k.\" Proof: P(X)=prod_i(v_i i^{-1}+X) and dual Q(X)=sum_{m in G}(1-(X-m)^k) agree at k+1 pts, both vanish at 0, equal => leading coeff 1 vs -|G| forces |G|=k. Lemma 4.3/Prop 4.4 lift this to p>k(k+1): every tuple over (1,2,...,k) mod p is (k,p,k+1)-proper. INTERPRETATION: this says the AP can be ROTATED (scalars s,r) into the all-interior box {1..k-1}^k mod p — i.e. a good runner-time EXISTS for AP-congruent tuples. It is an existence/solvability fact, NOT \"AP maximizes a covering measure.\" Does NOT transfer to L_y-extremality.\n\n(D) Tao 1701.02048 (closest transfer):\n - Prop 2 (trivial bound) delta_n >= 1/(2n) by Lebesgue: union of n Bohr sets each measure 2delta has measure <= 2n*delta. This IS a first-moment covering bound, structurally the SAME family as our L_y (a linear functional of covering counts).\n - SECOND-MOMENT input: when delta ~ 1/(2n) (near tight), the multiplicity (covering-count) function has L^2 norm >= (3/2 - o(1))^{1/2} > 1, forcing large L^3 and many TRIPLE overlaps B(v_i)cap B(v_j)cap B(v_k). This is a genuine factorial/second-moment argument on the covering-count distribution.\n - STRUCTURE lemma: near-extremal velocities \"must have a large structured component: there exists an arithmetic progression of length ~n containing ~n of the velocities.\" So AP-structure is FORCED at the extremum (via medium prime factors, Theorem 3: delta_n >= 1/(2n) + c log n/(n^2 (loglog n)^2)).\n - Goddyn-Wong extremal constructions sit in a bounded-length AP (matches tight-locus in our memory).\n\n(E) THE TRANSFERABLE LEMMA (precise, if any): Tao's moment method gives the template \"if the covering bound is near-tight then the covering-count distribution has anomalously high higher moments (clustering), which FORCES the velocity set into AP-structure.\" Translated to our setting this would read: \"if L_y(E) is near cap_k, the empty-sector count N has anomalously concentrated factorial moments S_r, forcing E to be AP-congruent.\" This is the CONVERSE direction we want (we want AP => max L_y; Tao gives near-max => AP-structure). It supports HYP-2607 as plausible/rigidity-flavored but does not directly prove it. NO lemma in any of these papers bounds L_y=E[g(N)] or proves consec maximizes a factorial-moment functional.",
        "remaining_gap": "No imported lemma proves HYP-2607 (consec maximizes L_y) directly. (1) The 2604.23906 sieve is a divisibility barrier with no measure content and stops at k=12, so it neither preempts nor helps LRC(14)=k=13. Their Prop 4.1 is AP-solvability, not AP-extremality of a measure. (2) Tao gives the only structurally matching tool — a second-moment clustering argument that FORCES AP-structure at the near-extremum — but it runs in the converse direction (near-max ==> AP) and is asymptotic (large n) with non-explicit constants, not an exact statement at k=8,9,10. To actually close HYP-2607 one would need to (a) adapt Tao's L^2-multiplicity inequality to an EXACT factorial-moment inequality for N at fixed small k, then (b) prove the rigidity converse \"only AP-congruent E attain the moment extremum\" — neither is in the literature. RECOMMENDED next step: try Tao's second-moment identity S_2 = E[C(N,2)] = sum_{|A|=2} J(A,E) as a Gram/quadratic form and test whether consec maximizes it subject to fixed S_1; our own data already says only S_4 is consec-extremal (S_1, S_2 are NOT), so a pure moment-extremality route likely fails — confirming the non-separability already recorded. The Tao import explains WHY (signed cancellation across moments) but does not repair it.",
        "verify": {
          "target": "Angle C literature-import claim (REFRAME-ONLY): characterization of the four 2026/2017 LRC sources and whether their AP-extremality / finite-reduction methods transfer to proving HYP-2607 (\"consec maximizes L_y = E[g(N)]\").",
          "verdict": "CONFIRMED",
          "evidence": "All load-bearing assertions verified against the primary sources and the exact breakpoint engine; the claim is a literature characterization (REFRAME-ONLY) that does NOT overclaim a proof.\n\nSCOPE (central claim, CONFIRMED): WebFetch of arXiv:2604.23906 confirms title \"Eleven, twelve, and thirteen lonely runners\" and that it proves k in {10,11,12} only, NOT 14 runners. Precursor arXiv:2511.22427 (\"Nine and ten lonely runners\") confirms the convention \"k+1 runners, k velocities\" (abstract: \"if k+1 runners start running... at least 1/(k+1) away\"). So 13 runners = k=12 (covered), LRC(14) = k=13 (NOT covered). LRC(14) is genuinely the first open case; this route is not preempted.\n\n(B) METHOD = divisibility-sieve barrier, not measure (CONFIRMED verbatim): Def 2.1 (k,p,l)-proper, Cor 2.5 finite reduction (J(k,p)=empty ==> p | u_1...u_k), and the polynomial-method Prop 4.1 all reproduced exactly from arXiv:2604.23906 HTML. No covering-volume / empty-sector functional anywhere — consistent with \"does not transfer to bounding L_y.\"\n\n(C) Prop 4.1 is existence-of-good-time, not extremality-of-AP-for-a-measure (CONFIRMED): Exact statement (\"Let k+1 be an odd prime. For all v in N_k there are s,r in Z_{k+1}^x with s v + r(1,...,k) in {1,...,k-1}^k\") and the P(X)=prod(v_i i^{-1}+X) / Q(X)=sum_m(1-(X-m)^k) leading-coefficient argument forcing |G|=k both verified verbatim. The interpretation (solvability/properness, NOT measure-maximization) is fair.\n\n(D) Tao arXiv:1701.02048 is the one transferable shape (CONFIRMED verbatim from blog): trivial 1/(2n) union bound; L^2 second-moment on the multiplicity function (\"L^2 norm... significantly larger than 1\" ==> large L^3 ==> \"triple intersections must be quite large\"); AP-structure forced at near-extremum (\"an arithmetic progression of length ~n that contains ~n of these velocities\"); Goddyn-Wong examples in a bounded-length AP, with the exact example (1,2,3,4,5,7,12).\n\n(E) HONEST DIRECTION CAVEAT (CONFIRMED, not overclaimed): Tao gives near-max ==> AP-structure (the CONVERSE of HYP-2607's needed AP ==> max L_y); the claim explicitly says it \"does not directly prove it\" and that no source bounds L_y=E[g(N)]. Logically correct.\n\nCOMPUTATIONAL BACKING: Ran 04-computation/lrc14_empty_sector_distribution_kps.py. Reproduces L_y(consec)=2633/7350~0.35823 (k=8), 26083/52920~0.49288 (k=9), 45253/79380~0.57008 (k=10), with consec beating every listed competitor and competitors leaking p_0 mass into lower-g sectors. Engine logic (breakpoints x=a/(7e), exact Fractions, g_poly) is correct.\n\nMINOR IMPRECISION (does NOT affect findings A-E; lives in the upstream \"VERIFIED facts\" preamble, not the claim): \"competitors LEAK p_0 mass into p_1 (g=0)\" is exact only for k=8 (g(1)=0); for k=9,10 g(1)=5/18 != 0 — the leak still lowers L_y because g(1)<g(0), but it is not g=0. Cosmetic only.\n\nThe claim's stated conclusion — sources located and characterized, their methods do not prove HYP-2607, Tao's moment method is the lone transferable (converse-direction) shape, LRC(14) unpreempted — is accurate and appropriately hedged. REFRAME-ONLY label is correct.\n\nRelevant file: C:\\\\Users\\\\Eliott\\\\Documents\\\\GitHub\\\\math\\\\04-computation\\\\lrc14_empty_sector_distribution_kps.py"
