        "result": "Exact verified facts (Fraction arithmetic, all breakpoint intervals, k=8,9,10). (1) consec p_0 = meas(S7): k=8: 481/1470=0.327211; k=9: 2447/5880=0.416156; k=10: 8899/17640=0.504478. Full distributions: consec_8 p=[481/1470, 359/1470, 25/147, 26/245, 17/210, 5/98, 1/49]; consec_9 p=[2447/5880, 653/2940, 27/196, 23/245, 13/196, 9/196, 1/56]; consec_10 p=[8899/17640, 551/2940, 127/1176, 145/1764, 55/882, 5/126, 1/63]. (NB: the prompt's guessed values 2402/7350 and the p_0=0.4162 estimate are consistent with 2447/5880; the 2402/7350 guess is WRONG.) (2) The dilated AP {0,2,4,...,2(k-1)} has IDENTICAL distribution to consec (exact, confirms THM-531 scale-invariance at distribution level, not just L_y). (3) consec UNIQUELY maximizes p_0=meas(S7) itself (not merely L_y) over all bounded-spread valid E (k=8: among 330 sets spread<=11, zero beat or tie consec's p_0 except the AP class). (4) Three-gap sufficiency: max orbit gap < 1/7 IMPLIES N=0 (all sectors hit), verified at every interval. But it is far from necessary: meas{maxgap<1/7} = 44/735, 47/294, 11/49 for k=8,9,10 — much smaller than meas(N=0). So the pure three-gap multiset does NOT determine p_0.",
        "proof_or_evidence": "For consec_k the orbit at x is the AP A_k(x)={0,x,2x,...,(k-1)x mod 1}. Sector j in 1..6 is missed iff A_k(x) puts no point in [j/7,(j+1)/7). Verified closed form N_consec(x) = sum over consecutive orbit-point spans [a,b) of #{j: a<=j/7 and (j+1)/7<=b} (the span-counting / gap formula) — matches direct N at ALL breakpoint midpoints for k=8,9,10 (script lrc14_threedist_consec_kps.py, output 05-knowledge/results/lrc14_threedist_consec_kps.out). Bucketing by floor(7*g_max): g_max<1/7 (bucket 0) gives N=0 at all 22 intervals (k=9) — three-gap uniformity sufficient. NEGATIVE results that close sub-routes: (a) NO pointwise coupling: N_consec(x) <= N_E(x) FAILS (30-98 violations per competitor) — kills the naive Sturmian/cutting-sequence pointwise-domination idea (angle B/E); the dilated AP {0,2,...} has identical distribution so can never pointwise-dominate. (b) NO first-order stochastic dominance: consec is NOT FOSD-smallest in N — fails for 119/120 (k=8), 44/45 (k=9) competitors, because competitors push extra mass into the high-N tail. Hence consec's L_y-maximality and meas(S7)-maximality are NOT explained by any monotone/stochastic-order argument; the extremality is genuinely SIGNED (consistent with the canon note that smallness is signed, and that g is non-monotone on {0..6}).",
        "remaining_gap": "The crux HYP-2607 is NOT closed. What is proved: (i) the gap-span closed form for N_consec; (ii) consec maximizes BOTH meas(S7) and L_y over bounded-spread (new: meas(S7) itself, stronger than before); (iii) elimination of three plausible proof mechanisms — pure three-gap-multiset determination of p_0, pointwise coupling domination, and FOSD. What remains open: a GLOBAL (unbounded-spread) proof that consec maximizes L_y, and a comparison principle. The three-gap theorem gives the gap MULTISET but p_0 depends on gap POSITIONS relative to the fixed j/7 grid, so three-distance alone is insufficient — any winning argument must track positions (an equidistribution/discrepancy argument against the 7-grid), not just gap lengths. Recommended next: combine the verified gap-span N-formula with an averaging-over-grid-phase argument, or pursue the signed stranger-contraction route (HYP-2610) which already captures the signed (1-r/7) structure that FOSD misses.",
